{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Moses called for all the elders of Israel and said to them</b> — This passage abbreviates what HaShem commanded him in the passage above, for it is well known that Moses said everything to Israel in detail and taught them the entire matter, and this is included in the verse \"as HaShem commanded Moses, so they did\" (Exodus 12:28). But Scripture mentioned this passage in summary, because <b>Moses called for all the elders of Israel</b> and they assembled all the people to him, and then they said to the entire congregation of Israel: <b>Draw out</b> the flock from the pasture to your houses, to be kept for you from the tenth of the month until the fourteenth. And it is possible that he said <b>draw out</b> because their flocks were very far from them in the land of Goshen, since \"every shepherd of flocks is an abomination to Egypt\" (Genesis 46:34). And he said <b>and take for yourselves</b> — take them <b>for your families</b>: a lamb per ancestral house, a lamb per household. <b>And slaughter the Passover</b> offering between the evenings, according to all that is specified above in the commandment.\n\nThey taught in the Mekhilta (here): <b>And Moses called for all the elders of Israel</b> — this teaches that a court performed the matter. <b>And he said to them</b> — the matter was from Moses' mouth, to say to all Israel; these are the words of Rabbi Yoshiya. Rabbi Yonatan says: the matter was from Moses' mouth, to say to the elders, and the elders to say to all Israel. Now according to Rabbi Yoshiya's view, <b>and Moses called for all the elders of Israel</b> means that they should assemble all the people to him, as I explained, and according to Rabbi Yonatan's view, they would say it to the congregation, and it would be \"speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel\" (Exodus 12:3) referring to the elders gathered with them, similar to \"and if from the eyes of the congregation\" (Numbers 15:24)." "<b>And He adds</b> in this passage an explanation regarding the application of the blood — that it be done with a bundle of hyssop and dipping into the basin — which was not stated explicitly above. But there (Exodus 12:7) it is stated without specification: \"and they shall take from the blood,\" to teach that every act of \"taking\" mentioned in the Torah without specification is by means of a bundle, and that every receiving of blood requires a vessel, as in the midrash of our Rabbis (in the Mekhilta here). And He explained to them in this passage (Exodus 12:22): <b>and you, let no man go out from the door of his house until morning</b>, because for this reason they were commanded to apply the blood upon the lintel — so that they would be protected there, as it says: \"and there shall be no plague among you to destroy\" (Exodus 12:13).\n\nRashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 12:22): <b>and you, let no man go out</b> — this teaches that once permission is granted to the destroyer to cause harm, he does not distinguish between righteous and wicked, and night is the domain of those who cause destruction, as it says (Psalms 104:20): \"wherein every beast of the forest creeps forth.\" And I do not understand his words in what he said, \"and night is the domain of those who cause destruction, as it says 'wherein every beast of the forest creeps forth'\" — is a person forbidden on every night to go out from the door of his house until morning based on this verse? He should rather have said that on this particular night permission was given to the destroyer to cause harm, and therefore He warned them against it. But the Rav did not see fit to say so, because the Holy One, blessed be He, in His own self and glory, was the one who struck.\n\nThis matter is taught in the Mekhilta (Pischa 11) in different language: <b>and you, let no man go out from the door of his house until morning</b> — this teaches that once permission is granted to the destroyer to cause harm, he does not distinguish between righteous and wicked, as it says (Isaiah 26:20): \"Go, my people, enter into your chambers,\" and it says (Ezekiel 21:8): \"Behold, I am against you, and I will draw forth My sword from its sheath, and I will cut off from you both righteous and wicked,\" and it says (Exodus 33:22): \"and it shall come to pass, while My glory passes by.\" This teaches that one should enter for good and depart for good. And so you find that the Patriarchs and the Prophets conducted themselves with proper conduct: \"and Abraham rose early in the morning\" (Genesis 22:3), \"and Jacob rose early in the morning\" (Genesis 28:18), \"and Moses rose early in the morning\" (Exodus 34:4), \"and Joshua rose early in the morning\" (Joshua 3:1), \"and Samuel rose early to meet Saul in the morning\" (I Samuel 15:12). And here is an a fortiori argument: if the Patriarchs and the Prophets, who went to do the will of Him who spoke and the world came into being, conducted themselves with proper conduct, how much more so all other people. And thus it says (Psalms 104): \"You appoint darkness and it becomes night, wherein all the beasts of the forest creep forth; the young lions roar after their prey... the sun rises, they gather themselves in\" — from here onward, \"man goes out to his work and to his labor until evening\" (ibid.). End of the baraita.\n\nAnd the meaning is that Scripture warned Israel in Egypt not to go out from the door of their houses on that night, because the Holy One, blessed be He, was passing through Egypt like a king who passes from place to place with his vanguard ahead of him, so that no man encounters Him or gazes upon Him — as in the matter of \"and HaShem my Elohim shall come, all the holy ones with You\" (Zechariah 14:5), and likewise \"and it shall come to pass, while My glory passes by, I will put you in a cleft of the rock\" (Exodus 33:22), to protect him from the angels of destruction and the heavenly entourage. And since we find that at the time permission is given to the destroyer to cause harm, he does not distinguish between righteous and wicked, therefore a person may not change the way of the world by going out at night, because it is the time of the beasts to which He gave them to prey, and no distinction can be made between righteous and wicked."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The verse cited as במדבר טו כד is Numbers 15:24, rendered as \"and if from the eyes of the congregation\" following the Hebrew literally.", 1 "The Hebrew ספקלטורים (spectatores/scouts) is rendered as 'vanguard' in context of the royal procession imagery; the word is a Greek loanword in Rabbinic Hebrew referring to an advance guard or attendants who clear the way."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:04.131255789Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}